# The TSF Timeline

## Quick History
After the collapse of the Confederacy and relapse of societies across the galaxy, multiple systems on the edge of the Perseus arm formed an alliance; In exchange for sharing their military assets, each system would ensure each other's colonies remained stable and secure amid the anarchy of the schism.
Eventually, as cultures intermingled, the alliance unified and expanded their governance in the local region, promising safety and security to all members.
This promise of safety and security, fueled by the trauma of the events of the schism, has grown into a desire to hold the galaxy in time, in the hopes that preventing another expansion will ultimately preserve more lives despite the bloody cost of the task. This has led to stagnation and slow yet steady economic decline in the Perseus arm.

## Longer History

### Confederate Origins

As the Great Confederate Expansion began to fizzle out in the Perseus arm, the Confederacy's once iron grip collapsed. This left hundreds of freshly colonized systems without government, and left behind anarchy, piracy, and mayhem as thousands of pockets of civilizations were let loose.
Many of these colonies, separated by long distances, established their own forms of government - some more civil than others - and sought independence from, at first, the oppressive Confederate regime they were left with, and then from newly-proclaimed empires.
Far into the Perseus arm, separated from the industrial base of the Orion arm, and freed from the Confederate military (which, at the time, lost cohesion due to the vast distances separating their command structure), multiple systems with civil governments
eventually formed a coalition known as the Persei Θ-Tellion 7 Alliance, named after the first (and largest) systems that first signed off on the agreement.
This alliance, with the good fortune of having local Confederate military secessionists, became particularly notable in the outer arm with its coalition fleet of reverse-engineered vessels, having an edge against the civilian-retrofitted fleets of their neighbors.
Over time, seeing the success and burgeoning power of the PΘT7A, multiple systems (some conquered) became further members of the agreement, expanding up to the Orion-Perses border void - limited by both logistics, and a lack of established colonies near the barren stellar landscape separating the arms.

### Unification

As the chaos of the Expansion settled out, with the PΘT7A conglomerate becoming spread out across a significant chunk of the arm, the need for stability nonetheless continued.
Having been in an alliance and in close proximity for years, the governments of the core Alliance worlds unified as one nation:
the Tellion Seven Federation (placeholder). The remaining edge-worlds were given some level of independence in return for becoming vassals of the Federation.
Of course, some systems were against this unification, but rebellions were quickly quelled by Federation security fleets under the premise of keeping stability.
Having unified a significant chunk of the Perseus arm, the final of the Federation was, ultimately to maintain their original alliance agreement and maintain stability across the many sectors under their control.

### Paranoia

Painfully aware of the human cost of the Confederate Expansion, the new precedent of stability at all costs began to take a toll on the systems under the Federation banner. Due to the long distances between edges of originally Alliance domain, each member of the agreement was
presupposed to contributing their military assets across each member's territory. While this worked excellently for the Alliance, serving as an effective way of keeping rogue members, pirates, and other anarchists from destabilizing any more of the galaxy, the unification of the systems
ultimately centralized control of originally allied assets to Federation control under the premise of strengthening the conglomerate military. While this was originally seen as crucial in keeping the peace (especially when confrontations hostile neighbors in the arm), it did not take long
for the vast military across previously-allied territory to become more than friendly peacekeepers.
This compounded with the Federation's core worlds' deep-rooted fear of losing control of their newly acquired territory, which was seen as very possible should even more stretching of their resources occur. (At this point, the Federation's logistic and communication capability was at its limit).
Due to the paranoia of another schism and the logistical limitations of the Federation, heavy regulations on sector colonization have been put in place (enforced by the ruthless navy spread across the worlds).

### Stagnation

The heavy expansion regulations put in place by the now authoritarian-adjacent federation, along with the sheer resource consumption of the many sectors under their control, has lead to scarcity across the outer arm. Less resource dense sectors have been mined out, and
economic recession has spread like wildfire. The rebellions, growing more commonplace as average citizens struggle to find ways to make ends meet, are put down with extreme prejudice by battleships once celebrated for their protective capability.
The once celebrated core worlds, still subconciously afraid of another great schism, are still convinced that the chaos of another galactic expansion justifies the harsh realities their citizens face.

### Today

Word has come from distant travelers originating from deeper in the arm of a new, prosperous sector filled with promise, not only of fresh minerals but also of scientific progress - even potentially a way to connect the galaxy in a way only dreamed of in science fiction.
These same travelers talk of the promises of a better future given by a nameless figure of lore, of the opportunities outside of the restrictions imposed by the federation and of the glorious power His followers wield.
Naturally, these rumors are likely folktale... but they have the core worlds concerned. Not only are the tactical and strategic values of the assets in this supposed goldmine worth consideration despite the possibility of being falsehoods, the coalescing noises of prayers to a better future, rebellions reaching closer and closer to the core worlds, and starving citizens
have the Federation afraid like they only were during the Confederate Expansion that birthed them.

Recently, a Federation task force has been sent to the Colossus sector, the ground zero for the whispered rumors that are appearing more and more likely to be true; They may be the last chance the Federation has to prolong the survival of the once-glorious Alliance.
